-- title:
--   Zero-scheme ideal of a section is isomorphism-invariant
-- statement:
--   Let $X$ be a scheme, and let $M$, $M'$ be objects of the category `X.Modules` of sheaves of $\mathcal O_X$-modules on $X$, viewed with its monoidal structure whose unit $\mathbb 1_{X.\mathrm{Modules}}$ is the structure sheaf. Let $s \colon \mathbb 1_{X.\mathrm{Modules}} \to M$ be a morphism, i.e. a global section of $M$, and let $f \colon M \to M'$ be a morphism which is an isomorphism. For such a section, `zeroSchemeIdeal` is the infimum, in the lattice `X.IdealSheafData` of ideal sheaf data on $X$, of all $J$ with the property that for every affine open $U$ of $X$ one has $\mathrm{coeffIdeal}(s, U) \le J.\mathrm{ideal}\,U$, where $\mathrm{coeffIdeal}(s,U)$ is the ideal of $\Gamma(X,U)$ generated by the image of the map `coeff s U` attached to $s$ on $U$. The assertion is that the ideal sheaf data of the composite $s$ followed by $f$ coincides with that of $s$: $$\mathrm{zeroSchemeIdeal}(s \gg f) = \mathrm{zeroSchemeIdeal}(s).$$
--
--   This is the statement that the vanishing (zero) scheme of a global section of a sheaf of modules depends only on the isomorphism class of the pair $(M,s)$ — classically, the divisor of zeros of a section of a line bundle is unaffected by transporting the section along an identification of line bundles, or by scaling by a global unit. It is used in the construction of polarisation frames and in the statement producing morphisms whose zero-scheme ideal has prescribed support on threefold tensor powers.
